{{smaller block|Whatever may be the fate of these attempts to ''visualize'' the physics of the process, it will still remain true that to account for the phenomena of radiation and absorption we must take into consideration the shape, size, and complexity of the molecules by which the ether is disturbed.
{{right|''Tyndall'', Radiation, § 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|Most persons . . . are less able to ''visualise'' the features of intimate friends than those of persons of whom they have caught only a single glance.
{{right|''F. Galton'', Inquiries into Human Faculty, p. 108.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To call up a mental image or picture with a distinctness approaching actual vision.

{{smaller block|I find that a few persons can, by what they often describe as a kind of touch-sight, ''visualise'' at the same moment all round the image of a solid body.
{{right|''F. Galton'', Inquiries into Human Faculty, p. 98.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is among uncivilised races that natural differences in the ''visualising'' faculty are most conspicuous. Many of them make carvings and rude illustrations, but only a few have the gift of carrying a picture in their mind’s eye, judging by the completeness and firmness of their designs, which show no trace of having been elaborated in that step-by-step manner which is characteristic of draughtsmen who are not natural artists.
{{right|''F. Galton'', Inquiries into Human Faculty, p. 101.}}}}

Also spelled ''visualise.''

